WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.758
Welcome to Part 4: how to wire it.

2
00:00:04.058 --> 00:00:09.380
By the end, you'll know the layout every job
uses, how to mark the plan before you start,

3
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:14.533
the order to do the work in, and what to
do when the house doesn't match the plan.

4
00:00:15.683 --> 00:00:20.672
Every job uses one layout. One
circuit, one breaker, kept apart.

5
00:00:20.972 --> 00:00:24.913
Each group of lights gets its own
feeder, from its own 4 amp breaker.

6
00:00:24.913 --> 00:00:27.447
The controller gets its own breaker too.

7
00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:30.514
At every injection
point, cut the positive.

8
00:00:30.514 --> 00:00:33.789
Power from one group
can't carry into the next.

9
00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:37.810
The data wire is never cut.
It runs all the way through,

10
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:39.921
so the pattern stays smooth.

11
00:00:41.071 --> 00:00:42.393
Why cut the positive?

12
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:46.760
Leave it joined, and power flows
from one group into the next.

13
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.487
Then one breaker ends up
feeding two or three groups.

14
00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:54.562
Cut it, and each group stands
alone. If one group faults,

15
00:00:54.562 --> 00:00:58.719
only its breaker trips, and
you know exactly where to look.

16
00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:02.878
Plan it before you start.
In the truck on the way,

17
00:01:02.878 --> 00:01:06.739
or on site before you unload.
Never figure it out as you go.

18
00:01:07.039 --> 00:01:10.432
Open the job photo from the
job files, and mark it up.

19
00:01:10.732 --> 00:01:14.361
Every roofline in the job,
and the feet of each run.

20
00:01:14.661 --> 00:01:16.632
A dot at every injection point,

21
00:01:16.632 --> 00:01:20.505
and the light count between
the dots. Never more than 70.

22
00:01:20.805 --> 00:01:22.260
And where the box goes.

23
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.768
Here the gaps are 40, 40, 38 and 42 feet.

24
00:01:26.768 --> 00:01:32.253
Even 42 feet is only 63
lights, so this plan works.

25
00:01:33.403 --> 00:01:35.476
On site, work in this order.

26
00:01:35.776 --> 00:01:40.684
One. Mount the box, and number the
breakers to match the dots on the photo.

27
00:01:40.984 --> 00:01:45.524
Two. Pull one feeder to each dot,
and label both ends with its number.

28
00:01:45.524 --> 00:01:48.822
Then nobody has to guess
which feeder is which.

29
00:01:49.122 --> 00:01:50.939
Three. Hang the lights.

30
00:01:51.239 --> 00:01:55.561
Four. At each dot, cut the positive,
and leave the data wire alone.

31
00:01:55.561 --> 00:02:02.647
Not sure which wire is the positive? Meter it before
you cut. Seal the cut end so it can't touch anything.

32
00:02:02.947 --> 00:02:08.022
Five. Land each feeder on its own
breaker, and the controller on its own.

33
00:02:08.322 --> 00:02:10.957
Six. Grease every connector as you go.

34
00:02:10.957 --> 00:02:14.328
Then power on, and meter
it before you leave.

35
00:02:15.478 --> 00:02:17.875
Sometimes the house
doesn't match the plan.

36
00:02:18.175 --> 00:02:23.249
Say the back run is longer than the photo
showed. Count the lights in that gap.

37
00:02:23.249 --> 00:02:27.866
If it's over 70, add a dot, with
its own feeder and its own breaker.

38
00:02:28.166 --> 00:02:30.473
Then update the photo in the job files,

39
00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:33.206
so the next crew sees what's really there.

40
00:02:33.506 --> 00:02:35.486
Out of circuits on the board? Stop,

41
00:02:35.486 --> 00:02:38.564
and call your crew lead
before you go any further.

42
00:02:39.714 --> 00:02:42.580
When you power on, the
lights tell you a lot.

43
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.734
One group dark, and the rest fine?
Look at that group's own breaker,

44
00:02:46.734 --> 00:02:49.138
then its feeder connection at the dot.

45
00:02:49.438 --> 00:02:53.776
Lights on, but the pattern goes wrong
from one point on? That's the data wire.

46
00:02:53.776 --> 00:02:57.085
Check the connection right
where it starts going wrong.

47
00:02:57.385 --> 00:03:01.803
Fix it, then power on again and
check every group before you meter.

48
00:03:02.953 --> 00:03:04.515
Two things are never OK.

49
00:03:04.815 --> 00:03:08.339
A run wired straight to the
supply, with no breaker.

50
00:03:08.639 --> 00:03:13.032
And two injection points off one
feeder, even if both groups are small.

51
00:03:13.332 --> 00:03:17.098
If a job seems to need either one,
stop, and call your crew lead.

52
00:03:17.098 --> 00:03:21.751
Your crew lead takes it up the chain
from there. There's always another way.

53
00:03:22.901 --> 00:03:23.925
Let's lock it in.

54
00:03:24.225 --> 00:03:28.000
Every group gets its own feeder
and its own 4 amp breaker.

55
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.722
Cut the positive, never the data wire.

56
00:03:31.022 --> 00:03:35.878
Mark the photo before you start. Never
more than 70 lights between dots.

57
00:03:36.178 --> 00:03:38.516
Number the breakers, label the feeders,

58
00:03:38.516 --> 00:03:41.823
and update the photo if
the house changes the plan.

59
00:03:42.123 --> 00:03:47.003
Dark group? Its breaker or feeder.
Pattern off? The data wire.

60
00:03:47.303 --> 00:03:51.521
Next, take the Part 4 quiz.
Part 5 is checking a box.
